  <!-- HINSDALE MASONRY PROFILE -->
  <section class="lp-section lp-profile" style="background:#E8E4DC;border-top:1px solid #D8D2C8;">
    <div class="lp-section-inner">
      <div class="lp-section-label">Hinsdale Architecture &amp; Masonry Profile</div>
      <h2 class="lp-section-h2">What Makes Hinsdale Masonry <em>Different</em></h2>
      <div class="lp-profile-grid">
        <div class="lp-profile-card">
          <div class="lp-profile-card-label">Primary Building Era</div>
          <div class="lp-profile-card-value">1880s – 1940s</div>
          <p class="lp-profile-card-desc">Victorian, Colonial Revival, and Prairie estates dominate Hinsdale's stock — all built with Chicago Common and hand-pressed brick in lime putty mortar that cannot be replaced with Portland cement without causing spalling.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="lp-profile-card">
          <div class="lp-profile-card-label">Dominant Brick Type</div>
          <div class="lp-profile-card-value">Chicago Common &amp; Hand-Pressed Face Brick</div>
          <p class="lp-profile-card-desc">Hand-pressed face brick and Chicago Common set in lime putty require specialist mortar matching. Modern cement-heavy mixes trap moisture and destroy the original brick face.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="lp-profile-card">
          <div class="lp-profile-card-label">Climate Risk</div>
          <div class="lp-profile-card-value">High — 90+ Freeze-Thaw Cycles/Year</div>
          <p class="lp-profile-card-desc">Chicagoland's 90+ annual freeze-thaw cycles force moisture deeper into deteriorated mortar joints with every winter — accelerating crumbling, spalling, and water infiltration behind the brick face.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- COMMON MASONRY PROBLEMS -->
  <section class="lp-section lp-problems" style="background:#F5F3EF;border-top:1px solid #D8D2C8;">
    <div class="lp-section-inner">
      <div class="lp-section-label">Common Masonry Problems in Hinsdale</div>
      <h2 class="lp-section-h2">What We Fix — <em>Most Often in Hinsdale</em></h2>
      <div class="lp-problems-grid">
        <div class="lp-problem-card">
          <div class="lp-problem-accent"></div>
          <h3>Lime Mortar Shrinkage on Pre-War Facades</h3>
          <p>Century-old lime mortar on Hinsdale's estates contracts and pulls from brick faces, opening gaps for water infiltration. Repointing requires a lime-based mortar matched in composition, color, and texture to the original.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="lp-problem-card">
          <div class="lp-problem-accent"></div>
          <h3>Tuckpointing Mismatch from Previous Contractors</h3>
          <p>Hard Portland cement on soft historic brick forces moisture into the face and causes spalling. Correcting incompatible prior repairs is a major part of our DuPage County Hinsdale work.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="lp-problem-card">
          <div class="lp-problem-accent"></div>
          <h3>Foundation Brick Deterioration at Grade</h3>
          <p>Grade-level brick on older Hinsdale estates fails first — ground moisture and freeze-thaw cycling combine to destroy joints and brick faces before the damage spreads upward.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="lp-problem-card">
          <div class="lp-problem-accent"></div>
          <h3>Limestone Sill and Lintel Joint Failure</h3>
          <p>Open joints on Hinsdale's limestone sills and lintels allow water behind the stone and into the wall assembly. Limestone repointing requires a different mortar mix than adjacent brick repair.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

